Q1. What is the Open/Closed Principle (OCP)?
Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

The Open/Closed Principle (OCP) states that

"Software entities (classes, modules, functions)
should be Open for Extension but Closed for Modification."

Meaning

Open for Extension

• New functionality can be added.

Closed for Modification

• Existing tested code should not be modified
  while adding new business features.

Why do we need OCP?

• Prevents breaking existing functionality.
• Reduces Regression Bugs.
• Improves Maintainability.
• Improves Scalability.
• Makes software easier to extend.

Real-Life Example

Smartphone

When a new application is installed,

the phone gains new functionality without
modifying the operating system.

The phone is

Open for Extension

Closed for Modification.

