Q2. How do Interfaces and Polymorphism help
implement OCP?

Interview Ready Answer

Interfaces

• Define a common contract.
• Existing interface remains unchanged.
• New classes implement the interface.

Example

interface Payment{

    void pay();
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("UPI Payment");
    }
}

Later,

class CardPayment implements Payment{

    public void pay(){

        System.out.println("Card Payment");
    }
}

No modification is made to the interface.

------------------------------------------------------------

Polymorphism

Client code works with the interface reference.

Payment payment = new UpiPayment();

payment.pay();

Later,

payment = new CardPayment();

payment.pay();

Later,

payment = new WalletPayment();

payment.pay();

The same statement

payment.pay();

works for every payment method.

JVM executes the correct implementation
using Runtime Polymorphism.

Memory Trick

Interface

↓

Defines WHAT should be done.

Polymorphism

↓

Decides WHO will do it.

OCP

↓

Add NEW implementations

without modifying

EXISTING code.

